    q: "Is Traulsen worth the premium price vs True or Beverage-Air?",
    a: "Honest answer from our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair across LA commercial service calls: yes for institutional and premium hospitality operators, no for fast-turnover chain restaurants. Traulsen lifespan tier (G-Series 12 to 15 years inland, R-Series 15 to 20 years, RIH 18 to 25+ years) substantially outlasts mid-tier True / Beverage-Air / Turbo-Air. For Marriott / Hilton / Hyatt hotel kitchens, hospital food service, university dining halls, military food service — operations where reliability matters more than upfront cost, where equipment downtime triggers serious compliance and operational consequences — Traulsen 'buy once, cry once' positioning is honest. For a fast-turnover chain restaurant cycling locations every 5 to 7 years, the Traulsen premium doesn't pay back. Traulsen typically outlasts 2 to 3 mid-tier replacement cycles. We service all brands and walk operators through the math honestly."
  },
  {
    q: "Should I repair or replace a year-15 Traulsen G-Series?",
    a: "Honest math we walk through with operators at the diagnostic visit. A new Traulsen G-Series reach-in retail runs $4,000 to $7,000 depending on configuration. Repair economics at year 15: compressor replacement $1,600 to $2,400 makes sense if the rest of the system is healthy (evaporator, condenser, controls) and the door gaskets are sound or replaceable. The Traulsen G-Series at year 15 is mid-life for a premium American commercial refrigerator — Marriott chain accounts routinely service G-Series through year 18 to 20 before unit-level replacement decisions. Replacement makes sense if compressor failure is the third major repair in 24 months, if the Smart-Eye controller has gone fully offline (some pre-2015 controller boards no longer manufactured), or if multiple gaskets plus condenser plus controls need simultaneous replacement. R-Series premium tier units routinely service through year 20+. Our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair tell you straight which call the math favors before any repair work begins."
